<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Context suspendWrappedResponseAfterForward="false">
  <!--
    Apply SameSite=Lax to every cookie the application sets (JSESSIONID and the app cookies
    userToken/visitorToken/cartToken/viewMode), which have no SameSite attribute otherwise.
    Lax (not Strict) is deliberate: it still sends the session cookie on top-level navigations
    from external links, so normal "click a link into the site" behavior is preserved, while
    blocking the cookie on cross-site subrequests, a CSRF-adjacent hardening (defense in depth
    alongside the existing synchronizer-token check).
  -->
  <CookieProcessor className="org.apache.tomcat.util.http.Rfc6265CookieProcessor" sameSiteCookies="lax" />
  <!--
    suspendWrappedResponseAfterForward: Tomcat suspends the response when a
    RequestDispatcher.forward() returns, discarding anything written afterwards. Every page here is
    rendered by PageServlet through a forward, so a filter that wraps the response cannot finish its
    work once the forward is done. ResponseCompressionFilter writes the tail of the gzip stream at
    that point; with Tomcat's default of true those bytes are dropped and the page arrives
    truncated, with no error on either side. Measured on the home page: the filter handed Tomcat
    4,770 bytes and 2,551 reached the client, and gunzip reported "unexpected end of file".

    Turning it off restores the behaviour response-wrapping filters rely on; Tomcat provides the
    switch for exactly this case. What it guards against is a servlet writing further content after
    a forward has already produced the response, which nothing here does. Servlets that write
    directly rather than forwarding (RobotsServlet, SitemapServlet) were unaffected either way,
    which is what identified the forward as the cause.
  -->
</Context>
